1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,120
Meet Adam and Sammy.
They're both thirty years old.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:07,000
They both earn fifty eight
thousand dollars a year.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,960
They've both got twelve
thousand dollars saved,

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,080
the first real money either
of them has ever had.

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:15,000
And they can both put aside two hundred

6
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,440
dollars a month,
every month, without fail.

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:23,900
Same age, same income,
same savings, same discipline.

8
00:00:24,806 --> 00:00:27,806
There is nothing separating these two men,

9
00:00:27,846 --> 00:00:33,726
except one thing:
what they believe money is for.

10
00:00:33,766 --> 00:00:36,686
Adam believes money should be safe.

11
00:00:36,726 --> 00:00:39,006
He watched his parents
lose a chunk of theirs,

12
00:00:39,046 --> 00:00:41,084
and he has no interest in repeating it.

13
00:00:42,054 --> 00:00:44,814
Sammy believes money should be working,

14
00:00:44,854 --> 00:00:48,574
that anything sitting still
is quietly going backwards.

15
00:00:48,614 --> 00:00:51,054
Thirty years from now,
one of them will have

16
00:00:51,094 --> 00:00:53,934
about a hundred and ten thousand dollars.

17
00:00:53,974 --> 00:00:58,174
The other will have around four
hundred and sixty nine thousand.

18
00:00:58,214 --> 00:01:03,996
Neither of them saved more than the other.
Not one dollar. Let's follow them.

19
00:01:07,014 --> 00:01:08,974
Adam opens a savings account.

20
00:01:09,014 --> 00:01:14,254
He gets a rate of about 1.5%, and he
does everything the responsible way.

21
00:01:14,294 --> 00:01:18,014
The $12,000 goes in,
and $200 is set up to move across

22
00:01:18,054 --> 00:01:21,934
automatically on payday,
so he never has to think about it.

23
00:01:21,974 --> 00:01:28,014
When his balance ticks over a round
number, he notices, and it feels good.

24
00:01:28,054 --> 00:01:30,492
The thing Adam likes
most about this account.

25
00:01:30,854 --> 00:01:35,374
and he'd tell you this himself,
is that the number only ever goes up.

26
00:01:35,414 --> 00:01:38,172
It has never once been
lower than the day before.

27
00:01:39,174 --> 00:01:44,494
For a man whose family lost money,
that is not a small feature.

28
00:01:44,534 --> 00:01:47,214
That's the whole product.

29
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:52,014
Sammy puts his $12,000 into a
total stock market index fund.

30
00:01:52,054 --> 00:01:57,614
Then he sets up the same $200 a month
automatically into the same fund.

31
00:01:57,654 --> 00:01:59,996
Then he closes the app.

32
00:02:00,518 --> 00:02:04,158
He owns a fractional slice of
several thousand businesses

33
00:02:04,198 --> 00:02:08,318
factories, shops, software
firms, logistics companies.

34
00:02:08,358 --> 00:02:12,398
He can't visit any of them.
He can't point at anything.

35
00:02:12,438 --> 00:02:14,460
There's nothing to show anyone.

36
00:02:15,366 --> 00:02:18,046
But crucially, he has
no idea what it'll be

37
00:02:18,086 --> 00:02:21,326
worth next year, and he has accepted that.

38
00:02:21,366 --> 00:02:23,406
His view is that over thirty years,

39
00:02:23,446 --> 00:02:26,686
the businesses will earn
money and he'll get a share.

40
00:02:26,726 --> 00:02:30,366
And everything in between is
noise he's not going to look at.

41
00:02:30,406 --> 00:02:33,326
Two men, same money.

42
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:37,340
One chose certainty, one chose ownership.

43
00:02:40,486 --> 00:02:45,212
On day one, Adam looks like the
sensible one. And it isn't close.

44
00:02:46,694 --> 00:02:49,854
His money is there all of it.

45
00:02:49,894 --> 00:02:52,814
He can log in and see
twelve thousand dollars,

46
00:02:52,854 --> 00:02:56,254
and tomorrow he'll see twelve
thousand and a bit more.

47
00:02:56,294 --> 00:02:59,612
If he needs it on Thursday,
it's there on Thursday.

48
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:05,790
There is no scenario in which Adam wakes
up with less than he went to sleep with.

49
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:08,270
Sammy logs in a week later and his twelve

50
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:11,790
thousand is worth eleven
thousand six hundred.

51
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:18,030
Nothing happened. No news,
no crisis. It just moved.

52
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:19,950
The way it moves every week.

53
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:24,092
And his $400 down for no reason
he could explain to anyone.

54
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:30,190
When their friends talk about money, Adam
has a straightforward answer. He's saving.

55
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:35,390
He's got a plan.
He's being careful. People nod.

56
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:40,030
Sammy says he's in the market
and the conversation moves on.

57
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:42,270
Because there's nothing to ask him about.

58
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:45,532
One of these men is doing
something that feels like control.

59
00:03:46,438 --> 00:03:49,758
The other is doing something
that feels like waiting.

60
00:03:49,798 --> 00:03:54,140
Three years in, the feeling
and the facts still agree.

61
00:03:59,302 --> 00:04:01,182
Let's stop and compare.

62
00:04:01,222 --> 00:04:04,782
Adam's twelve thousand has grown
to about twelve and a half.

63
00:04:04,822 --> 00:04:09,182
He's added seven thousand two
hundred in monthly deposits.

64
00:04:09,222 --> 00:04:14,062
Total nineteen thousand
nine hundred dollars.

65
00:04:14,102 --> 00:04:17,262
Every single month of
those three years the

66
00:04:17,302 --> 00:04:19,420
number was higher than the month before.

67
00:04:20,518 --> 00:04:25,278
36 months. 36 small wins.

68
00:04:25,318 --> 00:04:30,558
Sammy has put in exactly the
same $19,200 of his own money.

69
00:04:30,598 --> 00:04:34,798
And in year two, the market fell 35%.

70
00:04:34,838 --> 00:04:39,518
Sammy's total $14,600.

71
00:04:39,558 --> 00:04:43,964
He is $5,300 behind Adam. And.

72
00:04:44,038 --> 00:04:45,918
This is the part that matters.

73
00:04:45,958 --> 00:04:51,998
He is $4,600 below what
he actually put in.

74
00:04:52,038 --> 00:04:56,478
Three years of discipline,
and he has less than he deposited.

75
00:04:56,518 --> 00:04:59,998
This is the moment the
entire outcome is decided.

76
00:05:00,038 --> 00:05:05,020
And it has nothing to do with arithmetic.
Sammy's friends have opinions now.

77
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:09,038
His brother-in-law tells
him he's been sold a story.

78
00:05:09,078 --> 00:05:11,758
His father asks, kindly, whether he's

79
00:05:11,798 --> 00:05:14,478
thought about just
putting it somewhere safe.

80
00:05:14,518 --> 00:05:18,078
And every one of them can
point at Adam, who has never

81
00:05:18,118 --> 00:05:22,878
lost a dollar and who is
currently five thousand ahead.

82
00:05:22,918 --> 00:05:26,478
Most people sell here,
not because they're stupid,

83
00:05:26,518 --> 00:05:29,328
because the evidence in front
of them says they were wrong.

84
00:05:29,638 --> 00:05:33,838
The pain is real,
and the exit is one tap away.

85
00:05:33,878 --> 00:05:36,158
Sammy doesn't sell.

86
00:05:36,198 --> 00:05:38,718
He does something that sounds passive and

87
00:05:38,758 --> 00:05:41,918
is actually the hardest
thing in this video.

88
00:05:41,958 --> 00:05:47,758
He keeps sending $200 a month into an
investment that has lost him money.

89
00:05:47,798 --> 00:05:50,364
And here's what almost
nobody sees at the time.

90
00:05:51,494 --> 00:05:56,174
Those thirty six deposits Sammy made
while the market was down bought him more

91
00:05:56,214 --> 00:06:01,214
shares than they would have bought at any
other point in the whole thirty years.

92
00:06:01,254 --> 00:06:05,214
He was buying the same
businesses at a third off.

93
00:06:05,254 --> 00:06:07,614
It doesn't feel like an opportunity.

94
00:06:07,654 --> 00:06:10,332
It feels like a mistake
you're repeating monthly.

95
00:06:13,734 --> 00:06:18,014
Four more years. Adam is at
thirty one thousand dollars.

96
00:06:18,054 --> 00:06:24,414
Steady, predictable. Never once down.
Sammy is at forty thousand.

97
00:06:24,454 --> 00:06:29,148
Somewhere in year five,
quietly, the line crossed.

98
00:06:30,118 --> 00:06:36,158
There was no moment, no announcement.
Sammy didn't notice for months.

99
00:06:36,198 --> 00:06:40,638
But look at what actually happened here.
Because it wasn't the recovery.

100
00:06:40,678 --> 00:06:43,518
The market got back to where it started.

101
00:06:43,558 --> 00:06:46,558
That alone would have
put Sammy roughly level.

102
00:06:46,598 --> 00:06:50,876
What put him ahead were the shares
he bought while it was down.

103
00:06:52,102 --> 00:06:58,062
The crash didn't just cost him, it also
handed him four years of cheap buying.

104
00:06:58,102 --> 00:07:02,062
And he only collected on
that because he didn't stop.

105
00:07:02,102 --> 00:07:08,382
Now, Adam's position. This is where I
have to introduce the cost he can't see.

106
00:07:08,422 --> 00:07:11,868
Over those seven years, prices
rose about three percent a year.

107
00:07:12,422 --> 00:07:15,982
Adam's thirty one thousand dollars
buys what roughly twenty five

108
00:07:16,022 --> 00:07:19,502
thousand two hundred dollars
would have bought when he started.

109
00:07:19,542 --> 00:07:23,982
His account has never gone down.
His money has.

110
00:07:24,022 --> 00:07:28,142
Every single year, reliably,
with no chart, no crash,

111
00:07:28,182 --> 00:07:31,022
and no moment where anyone
told him it happened.

112
00:07:31,062 --> 00:07:33,820
Adam's protecting himself
from the risk he can see.

113
00:07:34,566 --> 00:07:37,486
he's fully exposed to the one he can't.

114
00:07:37,526 --> 00:07:39,326
And it's worth being clear about what he

115
00:07:39,366 --> 00:07:41,788
actually bought with that
one and a half percent.

116
00:07:42,918 --> 00:07:45,038
Adam lent his money to a bank.

117
00:07:45,078 --> 00:07:50,878
The bank lent it out again at six,
nine, twenty two percent.

118
00:07:50,918 --> 00:07:54,718
Adam supplied the raw material for
that business, took the risk that the

119
00:07:54,758 --> 00:08:00,158
bank might fail, and received one and
a half percent of what it produced.

120
00:08:00,198 --> 00:08:05,852
He is not a customer of that bank.
He's a supplier. The cheapest one it has.

121
00:08:09,126 --> 00:08:13,806
Year ten. And this is the checkpoint
where I want to be honest with you.

122
00:08:13,846 --> 00:08:16,926
Because the clean version
of this story isn't true.

123
00:08:16,966 --> 00:08:20,046
Adam is at thirty nine
thousand eight hundred dollars.

124
00:08:20,086 --> 00:08:25,116
Sammy, on paper, is at about seventy
eight thousand. Nearly double.

125
00:08:26,246 --> 00:08:32,286
Story over, you'd think. Except Year
10 also contains the second crash.

126
00:08:32,326 --> 00:08:34,686
And at the bottom of it, Sammy's account

127
00:08:34,726 --> 00:08:38,926
showed fifty two thousand dollars.

128
00:08:38,966 --> 00:08:44,206
So at that moment, and it lasted
months, not days, Sammy was

129
00:08:44,246 --> 00:08:48,060
twelve thousand ahead of a man
who had taken no risk at all.

130
00:08:49,126 --> 00:08:52,046
After ten years of accepting
every bit of volatility

131
00:08:52,086 --> 00:08:55,804
the market had,
ten years of stomach-turning,

132
00:08:56,950 --> 00:08:59,230
Twelve thousand dollars.

133
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:03,516
And here is the honest question:
the one most videos like this skip.

134
00:09:04,582 --> 00:09:07,262
Would you still be holding?

135
00:09:07,302 --> 00:09:11,182
Because at that trough Sammy
is being told, again by people

136
00:09:11,222 --> 00:09:15,662
who love him, that he has
spent a decade proving nothing.

137
00:09:15,702 --> 00:09:18,702
The gap is small enough
to look like noise.

138
00:09:18,742 --> 00:09:22,302
Selling would lock in a
perfectly respectable result.

139
00:09:22,342 --> 00:09:24,942
Every argument for
getting out is available,

140
00:09:24,982 --> 00:09:27,516
and several of them sound
extremely reasonable.

141
00:09:27,878 --> 00:09:31,838
At year ten I don't think either
of these men has clearly won.

142
00:09:31,878 --> 00:09:34,958
Adam has certainty and a slow leak.

143
00:09:34,998 --> 00:09:38,878
Sammy has a bigger number and
no idea whether he'll keep it.

144
00:09:38,918 --> 00:09:42,078
The entire difference between
them is still, at this point,

145
00:09:42,118 --> 00:09:47,518
a bet on Sammy's behaviour,
rather than on Sammy's arithmetic.

146
00:09:47,558 --> 00:09:52,060
That is the real reason most people
end up as Adam. Not ignorance.

147
00:09:52,294 --> 00:09:56,014
Almost everyone knows stocks
beat savings accounts.

148
00:09:56,054 --> 00:09:59,214
They end up as Adam because
being Sammy at year three

149
00:09:59,254 --> 00:10:03,774
and year ten is genuinely
physically unpleasant.

150
00:10:03,814 --> 00:10:08,254
And nobody warns you that the
unpleasantness is the price.

151
00:10:08,294 --> 00:10:12,494
That's what you're being paid
for, not cleverness. That

152
00:10:15,782 --> 00:10:18,302
Now step back from both of them.

153
00:10:18,342 --> 00:10:24,142
Because underneath this race, there's one
question, and it isn't stocks or savings.

154
00:10:24,182 --> 00:10:25,822
Adam is a lender.

155
00:10:25,862 --> 00:10:28,782
He handed his money to an
institution, took a fixed,

156
00:10:28,822 --> 00:10:32,862
small, predetermined payment,
and owns nothing.

157
00:10:32,902 --> 00:10:37,502
Whatever his money went on to do,
he gets the same one and a half percent.

158
00:10:37,542 --> 00:10:39,036
Sammy is an owner.

159
00:10:39,590 --> 00:10:43,070
He carries what real businesses
actually do, the good years

160
00:10:43,110 --> 00:10:47,550
and the catastrophes,
and receives what they actually earn.

161
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:54,030
That's the entire gap. Not risk tolerance,
not intelligence, not timing.

162
00:10:54,070 --> 00:10:59,790
One of them was paid for waiting,
and one of them was paid for owning.

163
00:10:59,830 --> 00:11:01,660
And once you see the distinction,

164
00:11:01,798 --> 00:11:05,278
you'll notice it's not a
modern financial idea at all.

165
00:11:05,318 --> 00:11:08,478
There's a body of law that's
spent about fourteen hundred years

166
00:11:08,518 --> 00:11:12,798
on exactly this line, and it
comes down hard on one side of it.

167
00:11:12,838 --> 00:11:14,652
Hold that thought for two minutes.

168
00:11:17,910 --> 00:11:21,390
Year thirty. Both men are sixty.

169
00:11:21,430 --> 00:11:25,470
Both have deposited exactly eighty four
thousand dollars of their own money.

170
00:11:25,510 --> 00:11:27,950
Twelve thousand at the
start and two hundred

171
00:11:27,990 --> 00:11:30,750
a month for three
hundred and sixty months.

172
00:11:30,790 --> 00:11:34,510
Not a dollar of difference. Adam.

173
00:11:34,550 --> 00:11:38,430
One hundred and nine
thousand six hundred dollars.

174
00:11:38,470 --> 00:11:41,724
Thirty years of saving
turned eighty four thousand

175
00:11:42,118 --> 00:11:43,324
Into a hundred and nine.

176
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:48,190
The account gave him twenty five
thousand six hundred dollars,

177
00:11:48,230 --> 00:11:52,590
about seventy one dollars a month,
averaged across three decades.

178
00:11:52,630 --> 00:11:55,230
And in real terms, after inflation,

179
00:11:55,270 --> 00:11:58,510
he has less buying power
than the day he started.

180
00:11:58,550 --> 00:12:03,710
Sammy four hundred and sixty eight
thousand nine hundred dollars.

181
00:12:03,750 --> 00:12:08,092
Three hundred and fifty nine
thousand three hundred dollars apart.

182
00:12:08,742 --> 00:12:11,262
And notice where that gap came from.

183
00:12:11,302 --> 00:12:15,022
At year three it was five
thousand, in Adam's favour.

184
00:12:15,062 --> 00:12:18,222
At year ten it was arguably nothing.

185
00:12:18,262 --> 00:12:23,342
More than half of Sammy's final number
arrived in the last eight years.

186
00:12:23,382 --> 00:12:26,542
Compounding does almost nothing for a very

187
00:12:26,582 --> 00:12:29,982
long time, and then
does almost everything.

188
00:12:30,022 --> 00:12:32,892
Which is exactly why the
choice is so easy to get wrong.

189
00:12:33,638 --> 00:12:37,692
The cost of getting it wrong isn't
visible until it's far too late to fix.

190
00:12:40,566 --> 00:12:44,206
Sammy got lucky.
You picked thirty good years.

191
00:12:44,246 --> 00:12:49,006
Run it over a bad thirty and
this video looks different. Fair.

192
00:12:49,046 --> 00:12:50,846
And I'll concede properly.

193
00:12:50,886 --> 00:12:55,886
Eight and a half percent is a long
run historical average. Not a promise.

194
00:12:55,926 --> 00:12:58,286
There are real thirty year stretches.

195
00:12:58,326 --> 00:13:01,006
Japan from nineteen
ninety is the obvious one,

196
00:13:01,046 --> 00:13:03,427
where that assumption would
have been badly wrong.

197
00:13:03,830 --> 00:13:07,790
Run Sammy at six percent instead,
and he finishes around two hundred

198
00:13:07,830 --> 00:13:12,590
and sixty eight thousand, rather
than four hundred and sixty nine.

199
00:13:12,630 --> 00:13:16,670
Still two and a half times
Adam, but the number moves,

200
00:13:16,710 --> 00:13:20,750
and anyone who tells you it
can't is selling something.

201
00:13:20,790 --> 00:13:25,710
Second concession: Adam's
certainty is worth real money.

202
00:13:25,750 --> 00:13:27,868
If you need your savings in four years,

203
00:13:27,926 --> 00:13:32,126
Adam's approach is correct
and Sammy's is reckless.

204
00:13:32,166 --> 00:13:35,726
Nothing in this video applies
to money you'll need soon.

205
00:13:35,766 --> 00:13:38,206
What the objection doesn't touch is that

206
00:13:38,246 --> 00:13:41,486
Adam's outcome required
no bad luck at all.

207
00:13:41,526 --> 00:13:46,766
He didn't get an unlucky thirty years.
He got a normal one.

208
00:13:46,806 --> 00:13:49,948
And finished with less buying
power than he started with.

209
00:13:50,438 --> 00:13:54,318
Sammy's downside needs a
historic disaster to appear.

210
00:13:54,358 --> 00:13:56,924
Adam's downside is the base case.

211
00:14:00,038 --> 00:14:03,438
There was a third man. Bilal.

212
00:14:03,478 --> 00:14:07,038
Same age, same fifty-eight
thousand salary,

213
00:14:07,078 --> 00:14:11,758
same twelve thousand saved,
same two hundred a month.

214
00:14:11,798 --> 00:14:17,038
He didn't like Adam's answer.
And he had a problem with Sammy's.

215
00:14:17,078 --> 00:14:22,364
His problem was this: Sammy owns
several thousand businesses,

216
00:14:22,678 --> 00:14:24,988
and he's never once
looked at what they are.

217
00:14:25,990 --> 00:14:28,670
A meaningful share of them
are banks and insurers

218
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:32,190
whose entire product is
lending money at a price.

219
00:14:32,230 --> 00:14:34,750
And Bilal thinks getting
rich off the interest

220
00:14:34,790 --> 00:14:39,070
system while complaining
about it is incoherent.

221
00:14:39,110 --> 00:14:43,230
So Bilal bought ownership
too, but screened.

222
00:14:43,270 --> 00:14:46,510
His fund excludes conventional
banking and insurance,

223
00:14:46,550 --> 00:14:49,660
alcohol, gambling, tobacco, weapons,

224
00:14:49,798 --> 00:14:54,238
And adult content. And it also
screens the balance sheet.

225
00:14:54,278 --> 00:14:56,398
Companies carrying interest bearing debt

226
00:14:56,438 --> 00:14:59,358
above about a third of
their value are out.

227
00:14:59,398 --> 00:15:02,718
Because owning them means owning the debt.

228
00:15:02,758 --> 00:15:07,038
This is the same principle
Sammy got right, made explicit.

229
00:15:07,078 --> 00:15:10,958
In Islamic finance,
you may not rent out money.

230
00:15:10,998 --> 00:15:13,916
That's riba, and it's prohibited outright.

231
00:15:14,438 --> 00:15:17,198
because a return should
attach to doing something

232
00:15:17,238 --> 00:15:20,958
and carrying its risk,
not to the passage of time.

233
00:15:20,998 --> 00:15:25,038
What you may do is own,
and take a share of what

234
00:15:25,078 --> 00:15:28,700
ownership genuinely produces,
losses included,

235
00:15:29,638 --> 00:15:35,598
The classical form is mudaraba. One side
brings capital, the other brings work.

236
00:15:35,638 --> 00:15:38,718
They agree a profit split
in advance, and if it

237
00:15:38,758 --> 00:15:42,158
loses money, the capital
provider bears the loss.

238
00:15:42,198 --> 00:15:45,758
Sammy's index fund is that
structure, by accident.

239
00:15:45,798 --> 00:15:50,798
Bilal's is that structure,
on purpose. And now the number.

240
00:15:50,838 --> 00:15:53,076
Because I'm not going to
give you the brochure.

241
00:15:53,366 --> 00:15:59,006
Bilal finished with $418,800.

242
00:15:59,046 --> 00:16:04,446
$50,100 behind Sammy. Screening costs you.

243
00:16:04,486 --> 00:16:07,806
Fewer companies, slightly
higher fees, and excluding

244
00:16:07,846 --> 00:16:11,726
financials means you sit out
the years when banks rally.

245
00:16:11,766 --> 00:16:16,636
In 2008, that exclusion
was a gift. In other years,

246
00:16:16,838 --> 00:16:18,318
It's a bill.

247
00:16:18,358 --> 00:16:23,678
Over thirty years, on these assumptions,
the bill was fifty thousand dollars.

248
00:16:23,718 --> 00:16:27,678
And there's also a purification
step a small share of income that

249
00:16:27,718 --> 00:16:32,158
came from incidental interest,
which you give away rather than keep.

250
00:16:32,198 --> 00:16:36,636
So Bilal didn't win.
He finished second, deliberately.

251
00:16:36,943 --> 00:16:38,958
and he'd tell you the
fifty thousand was the

252
00:16:38,998 --> 00:16:42,398
price of a portfolio he
could actually explain.

253
00:16:42,438 --> 00:16:47,678
He also finished three hundred and
nine thousand dollars ahead of Adam.

254
00:16:47,718 --> 00:16:49,838
Which is the number that matters.

255
00:16:49,878 --> 00:16:54,398
Because the real choice most
people face isn't Sammy v. Bilal.

256
00:16:54,438 --> 00:16:56,988
It's whether to be an owner at all.

257
00:17:00,102 --> 00:17:04,732
So what does it actually depend on?
Not intelligence.

258
00:17:05,894 --> 00:17:08,654
All three of these men
understood the options.

259
00:17:08,694 --> 00:17:11,534
It depends on whether you can
hold a position through year three

260
00:17:11,574 --> 00:17:15,854
and year ten, when everyone you
trust is telling you to stop.

261
00:17:15,894 --> 00:17:20,412
And on whether you want the last fifty
thousand or the clean conscience.

262
00:17:21,606 --> 00:17:25,886
Those are real trade-offs,
and they don't have one right answer.

263
00:17:25,926 --> 00:17:29,086
What isn't a trade-off is Adam.

264
00:17:29,126 --> 00:17:34,846
Adam took a guaranteed slow
loss and was told it was safety.

265
00:17:34,886 --> 00:17:38,766
Next time, two men buy the identical car,

266
00:17:38,806 --> 00:17:41,726
on the same day, for the same price.

267
00:17:41,766 --> 00:17:44,540
And one of them pays $8,000 more.

268
00:17:44,998 --> 00:17:47,868
Run the numbers, then run them halal.
